    "description" : "<figure class="inline-img"><figcaption class="caption">FILE PHOTO: A logo of HSBC is seen on its headquarters at the financial Central district in Hong Kong, China August 4, 2020. REUTERS/Tyrone Siu/File Photo</figcaption></figure><div id="story-body" class="story bot-15 relative" role="article"><p>HSBC Holdings Plc offloaded a roughly US$300mil piece of a loan to build a troubled New Mexico data center project at a steep discount, underscoring deepening anxiety surrounding the AI boom.  </p><p>The bank sold the slice of an US$18bil financing package - which funds construction of the campus in Doña Ana County tied to Oracle Corp. - at prices in the mid-80s, according to people familiar with the matter. The sale took place several weeks ago, shortly after Oracle sent a notice to the project’s developer citing force majeure, the people said, asking not to be identified discussing private information. </p><p>HSBC’s move comes as lenders weigh their exposure to AI debt alongside renewed regulatory pushback following a Democratic sweep. Jensen Huang’s abrupt departure from Nvidia Corp., a linchpin of AI’s rapid expansion, has also triggered concerns about the pace of AI growth.</p><p>The massive venture in southern New Mexico known as Project Jupiter has been beset by regulatory hurdles and opposition. Oracle sent the force majeure notice to the developer, a unit of Blue Owl Capital Inc., in an attempt to put off payments should the venture get derailed, Bloomberg reported in late September. ",
